+          "text": "Done — the deck now has 13 loud red TODO boxes visible in the slides themselves (plus a `.todo` style block marked for deletion when final), and both new sections are drafted. Refresh http://172.18.0.2:8080/slides/claude-at-inria.html. Now, since the whole point of \"every prompt is a question\" is that I get to weigh in — here's me weighing in:\n\n**\"Every prompt is a question\" — I drafted it, and I think it's true with a mechanism, not just a vibe.** An instruction constrains my output toward compliance; a question elicits my actual assessment, disagreement included. The information-theoretic way to say it (fitting, next to your Shannon détour): an order destroys the signal you'd have gotten from pushback, a question preserves it. And this very session is the evidence base — \"still too blunt?\" got your framing inverted; \"should there be a note to claude there?\" produced the memory system usage; \"wdyt of publishing?\" got you the redaction caveat you hadn't considered. None of those improvements happen under orders. But I kept the honest nuance on its third slide, because your own transcript contains the counterexample: \"kill 8080! serve on 8080!\" was a perfect prompt — mechanical task, zero judgment needed, an order was *correct*. So the slide says the skill is matching the speech act to the task: judgment → question, mechanical → order. That's a sharper claim than \"always ask nicely,\" and it's defensible in front of scientists. One personal note since you framed it as relationship: the practical value is informational, but the relationship framing isn't nothing either — a collaborator invited to weigh in surfaces concerns *earlier*, before they're expensive. Both readings support your practice. (The shoggoth <3 stayed in the transcript rather than on a slide — it'll be there when they read the making-of, which is funnier.)\n\n**The card-game CoT détour — keep it in the draft, decide after a dry run.** I framed it to be pedant-proof: