erminal, turnstiles/security scanner arch, waiting benches, potted plants, a big lobby holo-screen (welcome message, \"please queue\"), directory totem, vending machine, coffee corner, coat/charging lockers, floor logo inlay, elevator lobby with call panel (the first elevator!), velvet ropes/queue posts, a security camera (exists), a fire extinguisher, an ATM-like \"credit terminal\".\n\nStyle rules: same as the existing library — top-down, front face at +y with LEDs, top-left light and drop shadows, layered (before/after), animated where it helps (charging rings, barrier arm sway, billboard scroll, coffee steam), consistent palette. Then, once the props exist, floor 00's level + scenario (walk in unchallenged, the hall, the turn) is the natural next content step — the editor + scenario format can already do \"enter zone → dialogue → spawn wave → open exit\".\n\nSay if you want any of that changed; otherwise it goes as-is right after the current agent lands."}, {"t": "tool", "name": "✻", "target": "Waiting for 1 background agent to finish"}]}, {"role": "user", "text": "is the subagent stuck or something? why is time still going, but the tokens not pumping?"}, {"role": "assistant", "items": [{"t": "text", "text": "Let me check for signs of life without reading its transcript — is the log still growing, and are its processes (cargo, playwright, its server) running?"}, {"t": "tool", "name": "Bash", "target": "Ran 1 shell command"}, {"t": "text", "text": "Not stuck — actively working. Evidence: renderer.js was modified within the last 3 minutes (a Python edit of the pixel-art-rule comment/code was literally in flight when I looked), and it has already produced layers_fix_motes_px4/px6, layers_fix_screenwash, and layers_fix_stable_* screenshots — i.e. it's through the motes fix and the screen-wash inversion and is on the flicker/stability test. Its serve.py 8098 is up.\n\nThe \"tokens not pumping\" is just how the notification file works: the summary file (134 bytes) only gets written when it finishes; while it runs, its work shows up as file edits, builds and screenshots, not as text here. I'll get the completion ping when it's done — should be soon given where it is."}, {"t": "tool", "name": "✻", "target": "Waiting for 1 background agent to finish"}]}, {"role": "user", "text": "the check-e2e is slow.... do we have 2 systems, one running the engine part only in fake mode and one running the whole e2e thing? also, how does the editor work?"}, {"role": "assistant", "items": [{"t": "text", "text": "Yes to both, and the slowness has a specific cause.\n\nTwo test systems\n1. Headless engine gate — tests/playthrough_gate.rs drives src/sim.rs: the real ECS systems (movement, AI, combat, bullets, pickups…) in a native Rust loop with a scripted bot player, no browser, no rendering, no wasm. Runs in make check-test in ~0.5 s per floor. Its known limit: it doesn't run the scenario/elevator systems yet, so its \"win\" is still kill-all — a smoke test, not the real completion path.\n2. Full e2e — make check-e2e: rebuilds the wasm in release (LTO), installs bun deps, starts a server, runs Playwright/Chromium with SwiftShader software WebGL (3 specs, ~30–40 s). The slow part is almost never the tests: it's the release wasm rebuild (minutes when the cache is cold, and much worse when 2–3 agents' cargo builds fight over target/, which is what happened tonight). Two cheap speedups if you want them: make the wasm build a proper Make dependency (skip when nothing under src/ changed), and a make e2e-only target that just runs bunx playwright test against the already-built wasm.\n\nHow the editor works\n- tools/levels.html + levels-editor.js / -format.js / -diff.js is a plain vanilla-JS app (no build step) served by serve.py; ?viz → LEVELS just iframes it.\n- It reads levels/index.json and levels/floor_NN.json over HTTP; the canvas is the floor plan (walls/rooms/zones/spawns/pickups/entry/exits), the right panel is meta + the scenario steps; everything